\mt1 1 Timothy
\c 1
\s1 Paul’s Greeting to Timothy
\r (\ref 2 Timothy 1:1–2|2TI 1:1-2\ref*)
\p \v 1 Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by \add the\add* command of God our Savior and of Christ Jesus our hope,
\p \v 2 To Timothy, \add my\add* true child in \add the\add* faith:
\p Grace, mercy, \add and\add* peace from God \add the\add* Father and Christ Jesus our Lord.
\s1 Correcting False Teachers
\r (\ref Titus 1:10–16|TIT 1:10-16\ref*)
\p \v 3 As I urged you on my departure to Macedonia, you should stay on at Ephesus to instruct certain men not to teach false doctrines \v 4 \add or\add* devote themselves to myths and endless genealogies, which promote speculation rather than \add the\add* stewardship of God’s \add work\add*, which \add is\add* by faith.\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Literally \fqa rather than the stewardship of God in faith\f*
\p \v 5 The goal of \add our\add* instruction is \add the\add* love \add that comes\add* from a pure heart, a clear conscience, and a sincere faith. \v 6 Some have strayed from  \add these ways  and\add* turned aside to empty talk. \v 7 They want to be teachers of the law, \add but\add* they do not understand what they are saying \add or that\add* which they so confidently assert.
\p \v 8 Now we know that the law \add is\add* good, if one uses it legitimately. \v 9 We realize that law is not enacted for \add the\add* righteous, but for \add the\add* lawless and rebellious, for \add the\add* ungodly and sinful, for \add the\add* unholy and profane, for killers of father \add or\add* mother, for murderers, \v 10 for the sexually immoral, for homosexuals, for slave traders\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Or \fqa for kidnappers\f* \add and\add* liars \add and\add* perjurers, and for anyone else who is averse to sound teaching \v 11 that agrees with the glorious gospel of the blessed God, with which I have been entrusted.
\s1 God’s Grace to Paul
\p \v 12 I thank Christ Jesus our Lord, who has strengthened me, that He considered me faithful \add and\add* appointed \add me\add* to service. \v 13 I was formerly a blasphemer, a persecutor, and a violent \add man\add*; yet because I had acted in ignorance \add and\add* unbelief, I was shown mercy. \v 14 And the grace of our Lord overflowed \add to me\add*, along with \add the\add* faith and love that \add are\add* in Christ Jesus.
\p \v 15 \add This is a\add* trustworthy saying, worthy of full acceptance: Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, of whom I am \add the\add* worst. \v 16 But for this very reason I was shown mercy, so that in me, \add the\add* worst \add of sinners \add*, Christ Jesus might display \add His\add* perfect patience as an example to those who would believe in Him for eternal life. \v 17 Now to \add the\add* King eternal, immortal, \add and\add* invisible, \add the\add* only God, \add be\add* honor and glory forever \add and\add* ever. Amen.
\p \v 18 Timothy, \add my\add* child, I entrust you with this command in keeping with the previous prophecies about you, so that by them you may fight the good fight, \v 19 holding on to faith and a good conscience, which some have rejected \add and thereby\add* shipwrecked \add their\add* faith. \v 20 Among \add them\add* are Hymenaeus and Alexander, whom I have handed over to Satan to be taught not to blaspheme.
\c 2
\s1 A Call to Prayer
\p \v 1 First of all, then, I urge that petitions, prayers, intercessions, \add and\add* thanksgiving be offered for everyone— \v 2 for kings and all those in authority— so that we may lead tranquil and quiet lives in all godliness and dignity. \v 3 This \add is\add* good and pleasing in the sight of God our Savior, \v 4 who wants everyone to be saved and to come to \add the\add* knowledge of \add the\add* truth.
\p \v 5 For \add there is\add* one God, and \add there is\add* one mediator between God and men, \add the\add* man Christ Jesus, \v 6 who gave Himself \add as\add* a ransom for all — the testimony that was given at just the right time.
\p \v 7 For \add this reason\add* I was appointed as a preacher, an apostle, \add and\add* a faithful and true teacher of \add the\add* Gentiles. I am telling \add the\add* truth; \add I am\add* not lying \add about anything \add*. \v 8 Therefore I want the men everywhere\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Or \fqa in every place (of worship)\f* to pray, lifting up holy hands, without anger \add or\add* dissension.
\s1 Instructions to Women
\p \v 9 Likewise, \add I want the\add* women to adorn themselves with respectable apparel, with modesty, and \add with\add* self-control, not with braided hair or gold or pearls or expensive clothes, \v 10 but with good deeds, \add as\add* is proper for women \add who\add* profess to worship God.
\p \v 11 A woman\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Or \fqa wife\ft ; also in verse 12\f* must learn in quietness \add and\add* full submissiveness. \v 12 I do not permit a woman to teach \add or\add* to exercise authority over a man;\f + \fr 2:12 \ft Or \fqa over her husband\f* she is to remain quiet. \v 13 For Adam was formed first, and then Eve. \v 14 And \add it was\add* not Adam \add who\add* was deceived, but the woman \add who\add* was deceived \add and\add* fell into transgression. \v 15 Women, however, will be saved\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Literally \fqa She, however, will be saved\f* through childbearing, if they continue in faith, love, and holiness, with self-control.
\c 3
\s1 Qualifications for Overseers
\r (\ref Titus 1:5–9|TIT 1:5-9\ref*; \ref 1 Peter 5:1–4|1PE 5:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add This is\add* a trustworthy saying: If anyone aspires to be an overseer, he desires a noble task. \v 2 An overseer, then, must be above reproach, \add the\add* husband of but one wife,\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Or \fqa faithful to his wife\ft ; also in verse 12\f* temperate, self-controlled, respectable, hospitable, able to teach, \v 3 not dependent on wine, not violent but gentle, peaceable, \add and\add* free of the love of money.
\p \v 4 \add An overseer must\add* manage \add his\add* own household well \add and\add* keep \add his\add* children under control, with complete dignity. \v 5 \add For\add* if someone \add does\add* not know how to manage \add his\add* own household, \add how\add* can he care for \add the\add* church of God? \v 6 \add He must\add* not \add be\add* a recent convert, or he may become conceited \add and\add* fall under \add the same \add* condemnation as the devil. \v 7 Furthermore, he must have a good reputation with outsiders, so that he will not fall into disgrace and \add into the\add* snare of the devil.
\s1 Qualifications for Deacons
\r (\ref Acts 6:1–7|ACT 6:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 8 Deacons likewise \add must be\add* dignified, not double-tongued \add or\add* given to much wine \add or\add* greedy for money. \v 9 They must hold to the mystery of the faith with a clear conscience. \v 10 Additionally, they must first be tested. Then, if they are above reproach, let them serve as deacons.
\p \v 11 In the same way, \add the\add* women\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Or \fqa their wives\f* \add must be\add* dignified, not slanderers, \add but\add* temperate \add and\add* faithful in all things.
\p \v 12 A deacon must be \add the\add* husband of but one wife, a good manager \add of his\add* children and \add of his\add* own household. \v 13 For those who have served well \add as deacons\add* acquire for themselves a high standing and great confidence in \add the\add* faith that \add is\add* in Christ Jesus.
\s1 The Mystery of Godliness
\p \v 14 \add Although\add* I hope to come to you soon, I am writing you these things \v 15 in case I am delayed, so that you will know how each one must conduct himself in God’s household, which is \add the\add* church of \add the\add* living God, \add the\add* pillar and foundation of the truth.
\p \v 16 By common confession, the mystery of godliness is great:
\b 
\q1 \add He\add* appeared\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Literally \fqa He who appeared\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa God appeared\f* in \add the\add* flesh,
\q2 was vindicated by \add the\add* Spirit,\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Or \fqa vindicated in spirit\f*
\q1 was seen by angels,
\q2 was proclaimed among \add the\add* nations,
\q1 was believed in throughout \add the\add* world,
\q2 was taken up in glory.
\c 4
\s1 A Warning against Apostasy
\p \v 1 Now the Spirit expressly states that in later times some will abandon the faith to follow deceitful spirits and \add the\add* teachings of demons, \v 2 influenced by \add the\add* hypocrisy of liars, \add whose\add* consciences are seared with a hot iron.
\p \v 3 They will prohibit marriage \add and require\add* abstinence from certain foods that God has created to be received with thanksgiving by those who believe and know the truth. \v 4 For every creation of God \add is\add* good, and nothing that is received with thanksgiving should be rejected, \v 5 because it is sanctified by \add the\add* word of God and prayer.
\s1 A Good Servant of Jesus Christ
\p \v 6 By pointing out these things to the brothers, you will be a good servant of Christ Jesus, nourished by the words of faith and sound instruction that you have followed.
\p \v 7 But reject irreverent, silly myths. Instead, train yourself for godliness. \v 8 For physical exercise is of limited value, but godliness is valuable in every \add way\add*, holding promise for the present life and for the \add one\add* to come. \v 9 \add This is\add* a trustworthy saying, worthy of full acceptance.
\p \v 10 To this \add end\add* we labor and strive,\f + \fr 4:10 \ft SBL, BYZ, and TR \fqa and suffer reproach\f* because we have set our hope on \add the\add* living God, who is \add the\add* Savior of everyone, \add and\add* especially of those who believe. \v 11 Command and teach these things.
\p \v 12 \add Let\add* no one despise your youth, but set an example for the believers in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith, in purity. \v 13 Until I come, devote yourself to the public reading of Scripture, to exhortation, \add and\add* to teaching.
\p \v 14 \add Do\add* not neglect the gift \add that is\add* in you, which was given you through \add the\add* prophecy spoken \add over you\add* at \add the\add* laying on of the hands of the elders. \v 15 Be diligent in these matters \add and\add* absorbed in them, so that your progress will be evident to all. \v 16 Pay close attention to your \add life\add* and to \add your\add* teaching. Persevere in these things, for by so doing you will save both yourself and those who hear you.
\c 5
\s1 Reproof and Respect
\p \v 1 \add Do\add* not rebuke an older man, but appeal to \add him\add* as to a father.
\p \add Treat\add* younger \add men\add* as brothers, \v 2 older \add women\add* as mothers, \add and\add* younger \add women\add* as sisters, with absolute purity.
\s1 Honoring True Widows
\r (\ref Ruth 1:1–5|RUT 1:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 3 Honor \add the\add* widows who \add are\add* truly widows. \v 4 But if a widow has children or grandchildren, they must first learn to show godliness to \add their\add* own family and repay \add their\add* parents, for this is pleasing in the sight of God.
\p \v 5 \add The widow\add* who \add is\add* truly \add in need\add* and left all alone puts \add her\add* hope in God and continues night and day in \add her\add* petitions and prayers. \v 6 But she who lives for pleasure is dead \add even while she\add* is still alive.
\p \v 7 Give these instructions \add to the believers\add*, so that they will be above reproach. \v 8 If anyone does not provide for \add his\add* own, and especially \add his own\add* household, he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever.
\p \v 9 A widow should be enrolled if she is \add at least\add* sixty years \add old\add*, faithful to her husband, \v 10 \add and\add* well known for good deeds \add such as\add* bringing up children, entertaining strangers, washing \add the\add* feet of the saints, imparting relief to the afflicted, \add and\add* devoting herself to every good work.
\p \v 11 But refuse \add to enroll\add* younger widows. For when their passions draw them away from Christ, they will want to marry, \v 12 \add and thus\add* will incur judgment because they are setting aside \add their\add* first faith. \v 13 At the same time they will also learn \add to be\add* idle, going from house to house and \add being\add* not only idle, but also gossips and busybodies, discussing things they should not \add mention \add*.
\p \v 14 So I advise \add the\add* younger \add widows\add* to marry, have children, \add and\add* manage their households, denying the adversary occasion for slander. \v 15 For some have already turned aside to follow Satan.
\p \v 16 If any believing \add woman\add* has \add dependent\add* widows, she must assist them and not \add allow\add* the church to be burdened, so that it can help the widows \add who are\add* truly \add in need \add*.
\s1 Honoring Elders
\p \v 17 Elders who lead effectively are worthy of double honor, especially those who work hard at preaching and teaching. \v 18 For the Scripture says, “Do not muzzle an ox while it is treading out the grain,”\f + \fr 5:18 \ref Deuteronomy 25:4|DEU 25:4\ref*\f* and, “The worker \add is\add* worthy of his wages.”\f + \fr 5:18 \ref Luke 10:7|LUK 10:7\ref*; see also Leviticus 19:13 and Deuteronomy 24:14–15.\f*
\p \v 19 \add Do\add* not entertain an accusation against an elder, except on \add the testimony of\add* two or three witnesses. \v 20 But those who persist in sin should be rebuked in front of everyone, so that the others will stand in fear \add of sin\add*.
\s1 A Charge to Timothy
\p \v 21 I solemnly charge \add you\add* before God and Christ Jesus and the elect angels to maintain these \add principles\add* without bias, \add and\add* to do nothing out of partiality.
\p \v 22 Do not be too quick in the laying on of hands \add and thereby\add* share in \add the\add* sins of others. Keep yourself pure.
\p \v 23 Stop drinking \add only\add* water \add and\add* use a little wine instead, because of \add your\add* stomach and your frequent ailments.
\p \v 24 The sins of some men are obvious, going ahead of \add them\add* to judgment; but \add the sins\add* of others do \add not\add* surface until later. \v 25 In the same way, good deeds \add are\add* obvious, and even the \add ones that\add* are \add inconspicuous\add* cannot remain hidden.
\c 6
\s1 Serving with Honor
\r (\ref Ephesians 6:5–9|EPH 6:5-9\ref*; \ref Colossians 3:22–25|COL 3:22-25\ref*)
\p \v 1 All who are under \add the\add* yoke of slavery should regard \add their\add* masters \add as\add* fully worthy of honor, so that God’s name and \add our\add* teaching will not be discredited. \v 2 Those who have believing masters should not show disrespect because they are brothers, but should serve \add them\add* all the more, since those receiving \add their\add* good service are beloved believers. Teach and encourage these \add principles\add*.
\s1 Reject False Doctrines
\p \v 3 If anyone teaches another doctrine and disagrees \add with the\add* sound words of our Lord Jesus Christ and with godly teaching, \v 4 he is conceited \add and\add* understands nothing. Instead, \add he has\add* an unhealthy interest in controversies and disputes about words, out of which come envy, strife, abusive talk, evil suspicions, \v 5 \add and\add* constant friction between men of depraved mind \add who are\add* devoid of the truth. \add These men\add* regard godliness as a means of gain.\f + \fr 6:5 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa Withdraw yourself from such.\f*
\s1 Godliness with Contentment
\p \v 6 Of course, godliness with contentment is great gain. \v 7 For we brought nothing into the world, so\f + \fr 6:7 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa so certainly\f* we cannot carry anything \add out of it\add*. \v 8 But if we have food and clothing, we will be content with these.
\p \v 9 Those who want to be rich, however, fall into temptation and become ensnared by many foolish and harmful desires that plunge \add them\add* into ruin and destruction. \v 10 For the love of money is \add the\add* root of all kinds of evil. By craving \add it\add*, some have wandered away from the faith and pierced themselves with many sorrows.
\s1 Fight the Good Fight
\p \v 11 But you, O man of God, flee from these things and pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, perseverance, \add and\add* gentleness. \v 12 Fight the good fight of the faith. Take hold of the eternal life to which you were called \add when you made\add* the good confession before many witnesses.
\p \v 13 I charge you in the presence of God, who gives life to all things, and of Christ Jesus, who \add made\add* the good confession in His testimony before Pontius Pilate: \v 14 Keep \add this\add* commandment without stain \add or\add* reproach until the appearance of our Lord Jesus Christ, \v 15 which the blessed and only Sovereign \add One\add*— the King of kings and Lord of lords— will bring about in His own time. \v 16 \add He\add* alone is immortal \add and\add* dwells in unapproachable light. No one has ever seen \add Him\add*, nor can \add anyone\add* see \add Him\add*. To \add Him be\add* honor and eternal dominion! Amen.
\s1 A Charge to the Rich
\r (\ref Proverbs 23:1–5|PRO 23:1-5\ref*; \ref James 5:1–6|JAS 5:1-6\ref*)
\p \v 17 Instruct those who are rich in the present age not to be conceited \add and\add* not to put their hope in \add the\add* uncertainty of wealth, but in God, who richly provides all things for us to enjoy. \v 18 \add Instruct them\add* to do good, to be rich in good works, \add and\add* to be generous \add and\add* ready to share, \v 19 treasuring up for themselves a firm foundation for the future, so that they may take hold of that which is truly life.
\s1 Guard the Faith
\p \v 20 O Timothy, guard what has been entrusted \add to you\add*. Avoid irreverent, empty chatter and the opposing arguments of so-called “knowledge,” \v 21 which some have professed and \add thus\add* swerved away from the faith.
\p Grace \add be\add* with you all.\f + \fr 6:21 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa Amen.\f*
